/*! \page developer_backend_field_page Field and FFT Classes

\ref developer_backend_complex_page (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_math_page    (Next)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

The most important data structures used by the PSCF programs for 
periodic systems are arrays that store values of periodic fields 
(i.e., functions of position) on the nodes of a regular periodic 
mesh.  PSCF defines a set of class templates in namespace Pscf that 
represent such periodic fields and Fourier transforms of such 
fields. This namespace also contains a definition of a template for 
a class named Pscf::FFT that provides member functions that perform 
forward and inverse fast Fourier transform on such fields.  

The field and FFT class templates each take the dimension of space, denoted
by D, and a backend identifier class, denoted by T, as template parameters.
For example, the class template RField represents a real-valued field 
defined on the nodes of periodic mesh, and is declared as a template
\code
namespace Pscf {
   template <int D, class T> class RField;
}
\endcode
The specialization Pscf::RField \<3, CUT\> is thus a container for a 
real field defined on a 3-dimensional periodic mesh for which the 
underlying array is allocated in GPU memory.  A specialization 
Pscf::RField \<2, CPT\> instead a container for a 2D real field that 
allocates an array in CPU heap memory.

Four class templates named RField, RFieldDft, CField, and FFT are
used to store and manipulate periodic fields. All of these class
templates are defined in namespaces Pscf, and so the Pscf:: 
qualifier is often omitted in what follows.  For each choice of 
values for the integer D and backend identifier class T:

   - A instance of RField \<D, T\> object contains values of a 
     real-valued periodic field on the nodes of a regular periodic 
     D-dimensional mesh.

   - A instance of RFieldDft \<D, T\> object contains complex values 
     of the discrete Fourier transform (DFT) of a real-valued periodic 
     field.

   - An instance of CField \<D, T\> object can store either the 
     values of a complex-valued field on the nodes of a D-dimensional 
     periodic mesh, or the complex-valued DFT of such a field. 

   - An instance of FFT \<D,T\> provide an interface for D-dimensional
     fast Fourier transform operations. 

An instance of FFT \<D, T\> can transform a RField \<D,T\> to or 
from an RFieldDft \<D,T\>, or can apply a forward or inverse 
complex-to-complex Fourier transform to a CField \<D, T\>.

\section developer_backend_field_field_sec Field Class Templates

Specializations of the three field class templates RField, RFieldDft, and 
CField are all derived from corresponding specializations of the class 
template DeviceArray \< Data, T \> with the same argument for parameter T 
and an appropriate type for the elment type parameter Data. Specializations
of RField \<D, T\> with D=1, 2, or 3 are derived from specializations of 
DeviceArray \< Data, T \> in which Data is the appropriate type for real 
numbers used with the chosen backend, i.e., Data=double for T=Pscf::CPT 
or Data=cudaReal for T=Pscf::CUT.  Specializations of RFieldDft \< D, T \> 
and CField \< D, T \> are instead derived from specializations of 
DeviceArray \< Data, T \> in which Data is the corresponding complex 
type, which is Data=fftw_complex for T=Pscf::CPT or Data=cudaComplex 
for T=Pscf::CUT. 

The main difference between the field and DeviceArray class templates 
is that each field class templat stores information about the dimensions 
of the mesh on which the field is is defined, in addition to the actual
field data and the total number of elements. The vector of mesh dimensions 
is stored in each field class template (i.e., in RField, RFieldDft and 
CField) as a member variable named meshDimensions_ that is an instance 
of Pscf:: IntVec \<D\>, and that is returned by a public member function 
naned "meshDimensions".

A specialized class template named RFieldDft is used to hold the DFT of 
a real field because the number of complex values needed to represent 
the DFT of a real field is approximately half the number of nodes in 
the real-space mesh on which the original field was defined. 
The number of elements array used by RFieldDft to store values of the 
DFT is thus different from the number elements in the corresponding 
real-space mesh, and is not simply equal to the produce of the mesh
dimensions. Instances of CField \<D, T\> with the same mesh dimensions 
can, however, be used to store either a complex field or its DFT, 
because the DFT of a complex-valued field is stored on a Fourier-space 
mesh with the same dimensions as the corresponding real-space mesh.

For each field class templates, explicit partial specializations are
defined for each allowed value of the backend identifier class parameter
T.  The directory src/pscf/field/cuda contains a set of partial 
specializations of these classes for a template argument T=Pscf::CUT, 
which are written in CUDA C++ and enable use of a GPU.  The directory 
src/pscf/field/cpu contains an analogous set of partial specializations 
for a template argument T=Pscf::CPT, which are written in standard C++ 
and use standard CPU hardware.  

For example, the partial specialization of RField \<D,T\> for T=Pscf::CPT 
is defined in the file pscf/field/cpu/RField.h as a template
\code
namespace Pscf {

    template <int D>
    class RField<D, CPT> : public DeviceArray<double,CPT>
    {
    public:

        // Default constructor
        RField();

        // Destructor.
        ~RField() override;

        // Allocator that also sets mesh dimensions.
        allocate(IntVec<D> const & meshDimensions);

        // Deallocate the array nullify mesh dimensions.
        deallocate();

        // Return the mesh dimensions.
        IntVec<D> const & meshDimensions() const;

        // ...

    private:

        IntVec<D> meshDimensions_;

        // ...

    }

}
\endcode
This defines a container that owns a block of CPU main memory. The 
corresponding partial specialization of RField \<D,T\> for T=Pscf::CUT 
is defined in file pscf/field/cuda/RField.h, and is derived from 
DeviceArray<cudaReal,Pscf::CUT>, thus creating a container that owns 
a block of GPU global memory. The allocate member function for either 
partial specialization of RField takes the IntVec<D> meshDimensions
as an argument and allocates an array in which the number of elements 
is equal to the product of the D elements of this vector, corresponding
to the number of nodes in the resulting mesh.

Specializations of the field class templates with T=Pscf::CPT are 
indirectly derived from the Util::Array class template, because the
partial specialization DeviceArray \<Data, Pscf::CPT \> is derived 
from Util::Array \< Data \>. Specializations that use C++ hardware
thus inherit the overloaded subscript (square bracket) operator that 
is defined by the Util::Array template, which provides access to 
individual array elements from within code that runs on the CPU. 
Specializations of the field classes with T=Pscf::CUT instead allow 
access to individual elements within CUDA kernels that can access 
GPU global memory.

\section developer_backend_field_fft_sec FFT Class Templates

Partial specializations of the template FFT \<D,T\> with arguments
T=Pscf::CPT and T=Pscf::CUT are wrappers around different external
FFT libraries.  

An instance of FFT \<D, CPT\> with D=1,2, or 3 is a wrapper for the 
open-source FFTW library, which uses standard CPU hardware. This 
partial specialization is defined in the file pscf/field/cpp/FFT.h .

An instance of Pscf::Cuda::FFT \<D, CUT\> with D=1, 2, or 3 is instead 
a wrapper around the NVIDIA cufft library, which performs FFT operations 
on a GPU. This partial specialization is defined in the file
pscf/field/cuda/FFT.h.

Specializations of the FFT template that use different backends provide 
analogous interfaces for forward and inverse D-dimensional transforms, in 
which specializations of the field class templates are used as function 
parameter types. For either allowed value of T, the partial specialization 
FFT \<D, T\> defines member functions that perform forward real-to-complex 
transform and inverse complex-to-real transforms with signatures of the 
form
\code
   void forwardTransform(RField<D,T> const & in, RFieldDft<D,T> & out) const;
   void inverseTransform(RFieldDft<D,T> const & in, RField<D,T> & out) const;
\endcode
Each partial specialization of the FFT template also defines a pair of
functions for forward and inverse transformations on complex-valued fields,
with signatures of the form
\code
   void forwardTransform(CField<D,T> const & in, CField<D,T> & out) const;
   void inverseTransform(CField<D,T> const & in, CField<D,T> & out) const;
\endcode
For each of these pairs of functions, "round-trip" application of the
forwardTransform and inverseTransform functions returns the original field.
The use of analogous function interfaces by specializations of the FFT 
template that use different backends allow these specializations to be 
used interchangeably within function templates that take a backend
identifier class as a template parameter. 

<BR>
